\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Why the annealer freezes and surgery does not. The landscape is the waist cut weight $w$ over contraction trees, with neighboring trees connected by the annealer's local rewrites; each rewrite moves one subtree across the waist bipartition, so a distinct separator $(A',B')$ of comparable balance is reachable only through a long sequence of mixed bipartitions of higher cut weight, which a late-schedule annealer never accepts (dotted attempt). Surgery side-steps the barrier by improving the cut in cut space---bounded FM, seeded to jump separators (Sec.~\ref {sec:frozenwaist})---and rebuilding both sides (Algorithm~\ref {alg:surgery}, lines 5--8). The well-depth gap is not hypothetical: it is $3$--$12$ bits on every one of the 835 instrumented calls (Fig.~\ref {fig:board}a). Schematic.}}{6}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:escape}{{1}{6}{Why the annealer freezes and surgery does not. The landscape is the waist cut weight $w$ over contraction trees, with neighboring trees connected by the annealer's local rewrites; each rewrite moves one subtree across the waist bipartition, so a distinct separator $(A',B')$ of comparable balance is reachable only through a long sequence of mixed bipartitions of higher cut weight, which a late-schedule annealer never accepts (dotted attempt). Surgery side-steps the barrier by improving the cut in cut space---bounded FM, seeded to jump separators (Sec.~\ref {sec:frozenwaist})---and rebuilding both sides (Algorithm~\ref {alg:surgery}, lines 5--8). The well-depth gap is not hypothetical: it is $3$--$12$ bits on every one of the 835 instrumented calls (Fig.~\ref {fig:board}a). Schematic}{figure.caption.6}{}}
